L000521	\section{Pathwise interpretation of the universal bounds}
L000522	
L000523	The universal bounds on the DRM MFPT have a simple pathwise interpretation. The key idea is to label searchers according to whether a recruited searcher, in the absence of other searchers, could eventually find the target before dying. This
L000524	labeling separates the time spent waiting for a potentially successful searcher from the time that this searcher subsequently needs to find the target.
L000525	
L000526	Let $\sigma_n$ denote the recruitment time of searcher $n$, with $\sigma_0=0$ for the initial searcher and
L000527	\begin{equation}
L000528	\sigma_n=\xi_1+\cdots+\xi_n
L000529	\end{equation}
L000530	for $n\ge 1$ where $\xi_i\sim \textrm{Exp}(\lambda)$.
L000531	Let $\tau_n$ denote the intrinsic FPT of searcher $n$, measured from its own
L000532	recruitment time and let $D_n\sim \textrm{Exp}(\mu)$ denote its intrinsic lifetime. We assume the variables $\{\xi_i\}_{i\geq 0}$, $\{\tau_i\}_{i\geq 0}$, and $\{D_i\}_{i\geq 0}$ are mutually independent.
L000533	
L000534	We call searcher $n$ successful if it finds the target before dying, \emph{i.e.}, if $\tau_n<D_n$. Now define
L000535	\begin{equation}
L000536	N:=\min\{n\ge 0:\tau_n<D_n\},
L000537	\label{eq:first_successful_index}
L000538	\end{equation}
L000539	the index of the first successful searcher in order of recruitment. Importantly, this is not necessarily the searcher that realizes the DRM FPT. A later recruit may be born after $\sigma_N$ and still overtake searcher $N$ by hitting the target sooner in absolute time. Nevertheless searcher $N$ gives a useful pathwise comparison.
L000540	
L000541	Since no unsuccessful searcher finds the target before dying, and since no searcher can find
L000542	the target before it has been recruited, we have the pathwise bounds
L000543	\begin{equation}
L000544	\sigma_N \le T_{\lambda,\mu} \le \sigma_N+\tau_N,
L000545	\label{eq:pathwise_sandwich}
L000546	\end{equation}
L000547	which contain the probabilistic content of both bounds on the DRM MFPT.
L000548	\\
L000549	\subsection{The lower bound as the birth time of the first successful searcher}
L000550	
L000551	The left inequality in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pathwise_sandwich} says that the DRM process cannot find
L000552	the target before the first eventually successful searcher has been recruited. Thus the lower
L000553	bound corresponds to an optimistic auxiliary process in which every searcher satisfying
L000554	$\tau_n<D_n$ is declared to find the target immediately upon recruitment. The success indicators of all the recruits are i.i.d.\ Bernoulli random variables
L000555	with parameter $p_\mu$ given by
L000556	\begin{equation}
L000557	p_\mu
L000558	=
L000559	\mathbb P(\tau<D)
L000560	=
L000561	\int_0^\infty e^{-\mu t} \mathbb P(\tau = t
L000562	)\,\textup{d}t.
L000563	\label{eq:prob_success_probability}
L000564	\end{equation}
L000565	
L000566	Hence $N$ as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:first_successful_index} is geometric on $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ with mean
L000567	\begin{align}
L000568	\mathbb E[N]
L000569	&=
L000570	\frac{1-p_\mu}{p_\mu}.
L000571	\end{align}
L000572	Since $N$ is independent of the recruitment times, we obtain
L000573	\begin{align}
L000574	\mathbb E[\sigma_N]
L000575	=\frac{1}{\lambda}\mathbb E[N]
L000576	=
L000577	\frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}.
L000578	\end{align}
L000579	Taking expectations in the left inequality of Eq.~\eqref{eq:pathwise_sandwich} therefore gives
L000580	\begin{equation}
L000581	\mathbb E[T_{\lambda,\mu}]
L000582	\ge
L000583	\frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}.
L000584	\label{eq:probabilistic_lower_bound}
L000585	\end{equation}
L000586	
L000587	\subsection{The upper bound is obtained by following the first-born successful searcher}
L000588	
L000589	The right inequality in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pathwise_sandwich} gives the upper bound. Once the
L000590	first successful searcher in recruitment order has been identified, one possible way for the
L000591	DRM process to find the target is simply to wait until this particular searcher finds it.
L000592	The actual process can only be faster, since any other successful searcher may also find the
L000593	target first.
L000594	
L000595	Taking expectations in the right inequality of Eq.~\eqref{eq:pathwise_sandwich} gives
L000596	\begin{equation}
L000597	\mathbb E[T_{\lambda,\mu}]
L000598	\le
L000599	\mathbb E[\sigma_N]+\mathbb E[\tau_N].
L000600	\end{equation}
L000601	The first term is known. The second term is the mean hitting time
L000602	of a searcher conditioned on the event that it succeeds before dying. Indeed,
L000603	\begin{equation}
L000604	\mathbb P(\tau = t \mid \tau<D)
L000605	= \frac{e^{-\mu t}f_\tau(t)}{p_\mu}\,\textup{d}t.
L000606	\end{equation}
L000607	Hence
L000608	\begin{align}
L000609	\mathbb E[\tau_N]
L000610	=
L000611	\frac{\int_0^\infty t e^{-\mu t}f_\tau(t)\,\textup{d}t}{p_\mu}
L000612	=
L000613	-\frac{\partial_\mu p_\mu}{p_\mu}
L000614	=
L000615	-\partial_\mu \log p_\mu.
L000616	\end{align}
L000617	Combining this with the expression for $\mathbb E[\sigma_N]$ gives
L000618	\begin{equation}
L000619	\mathbb E[T_{\lambda,\mu}] \leq \frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu} - \partial_\mu \log p_\mu.
L000620	\label{eq:probabilistic_upper_bound}
L000621	\end{equation}
L000622	
L000623	
L000624	
L000625	\section{Survival probability approach for the universal bounds}
L000626	
L000627	\subsection{Optimal lower bound for the DRM MFPT}
L000628	The DRM MFPT can be expressed in terms of the corresponding survival probability,
L000629	\begin{equation} \label{firstmean}
L000630	    \mathbb{E}[T_{\lambda,\mu}]
L000631	    =
L000632	    \int_0^\infty S_{\lambda,\mu}(t)\,\mathrm{d}t.
L000633	\end{equation}
L000634	Since $S_{0,\mu}(t) \geq 1 - p_\mu$ for all $t>0$ with $p_\mu$ as defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:prob_success_probability}, substitution into Eq.~\eqref{final} and subsequently into Eq.~\eqref{firstmean} reproduces the universal lower bound from Sect.~S2.A. given by
L000635	\begin{align}
L000636	    \mathbb{E}[T_{\lambda,\mu}] \geq \frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}.
L000637	\end{align}
L000638	
L000639	\subsection{Optimal upper bound for the DRM MFPT}
L000640	To determine an optimal upper bound on the MFPT, we first define a useful function, namely
L000641	\begin{equation} \label{Rmu}
L000642	    R_\mu(t)
L000643	    :=
L000644	    \int_t^\infty e^{-\mu t'}\, \mathbb P(\tau=t')\,\mathrm{d}t' ,
L000645	\end{equation}
L000646	which is clearly bounded, $R_\mu(t)\in[0,p_\mu]$. It immediately follows that
L000647	\begin{equation} \label{s0mu}
L000648	    S_{0,\mu}(t)
L000649	    =
L000650	    1-p_\mu+R_\mu(t).
L000651	\end{equation}
L000652	Using Eq.~\eqref{s0mu} and defining $G_\mu(t)$ as the FPT survival probability of a DRM process initialized empty,
L000653	\begin{equation}
L000654	    G_\mu(t):=
L000655	    \exp\!\left(-\lambda\int_0^t \bigl(p_\mu-R_\mu(t')\bigr)\,\textrm{d}t'\right),
L000656	    \label{eq:Gdef}
L000657	\end{equation}
L000658	we can rewrite Eq.~\eqref{firstmean} as
L000659	\begin{align}
L000660	\mathbb{E}[T_{\lambda,\mu}]
L000661	&=
L000662	\frac{1-p_\mu}{p_\mu}
L000663	\int_0^\infty \bigl(p_\mu-R_\mu(t)\bigr)G_\mu(t)\,\mathrm{d}t
L000664	+
L000665	\frac{1}{p_\mu}\int_0^\infty R_\mu(t)G_\mu(t)\,\mathrm{d}t.
L000666	\label{eq:MFPT_decomp}
L000667	\end{align}
L000668	The first term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:MFPT_decomp} can be simplified,
L000669	\begin{align}
L000670	\frac{1-p_\mu}{p_\mu}
L000671	\int_0^\infty \bigl(p_\mu-R_\mu(t)\bigr)G_\mu(t)\,\mathrm{d}t
L000672	=
L000673	-\frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}
L000674	\int_0^\infty G_\mu'(t)\,\mathrm{d}t =
L000675	\frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}
L000676	\bigl(G_\mu(0)-G_\mu(\infty)\bigr) =
L000677	\frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}.
L000678	\label{eq:first_term_exact}
L000679	\end{align}
L000680	For the second term, we use that $G_\mu(t)\in[0,1]$,
L000681	\begin{equation}
L000682	\frac{1}{p_\mu}\int_0^\infty R_\mu(t)G_\mu(t)\,\mathrm{d}t
L000683	\le
L000684	\frac{1}{p_\mu}\int_0^\infty R_\mu(t)\,\mathrm{d}t,
L000685	\end{equation}
L000686	and finally that
L000687	\begin{align}
L000688	    \int_0^\infty R_\mu(t)\,\mathrm{d}t
L000689	    =
L000690	    -\frac{\partial p_\mu}{\partial \mu}.
L000691	    \label{eq:q_derivative}
L000692	\end{align}
L000693	Combining the preceding equations gives
L000694	\begin{equation}
L000695	    \mathbb{E}[T_{\lambda,\mu}]
L000696	    \le
L000697	    \frac{1-p_\mu}{\lambda p_\mu}
L000698	    -
L000699	    \frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu,
L000700	    \label{eq:upper_bound}
L000701	\end{equation}
L000702	reproducing the universal upper bound from Sect.~S2.B.
L000703	
L000704	
L000705	\section{When does $-\partial_\mu \log p_\mu \to 0$?}
L000706	
L000707	Based on the probabilistic derivation of the bounds, the second term in the upper bound,
L000708	\begin{equation}
L000709	    -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu,
L000710	\end{equation}
L000711	has a simple interpretation. Indeed, it is the mean first-passage time of a single searcher conditioned on finding the target before dying. This interpretation also allows us to see when the second term vanishes. Let
L000712	\begin{equation}
L000713	    t_* :=
L000714	    \inf\{t\ge 0: \mathbb P(\tau = t)>0\}
L000715	\end{equation}
L000716	be the left edge of the support of the first-passage time density. As $\mu\to\infty$, the exponential tilt of the conditioned first-passage time density increasingly selects the fastest possible successful trajectories, and hence
L000717	\begin{equation}
L000718	    -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu
L000719	    \to
L000720	    t_*.
L000721	    \label{eq:second_term_limit}
L000722	\end{equation}
L000723	Therefore, whenever there is no hard cutoff on travel time to the target and arbitrarily short first-passage times are possible (e.g. in Brownian diffusion), $t_* \to 0$, and consequently,
L000724	\begin{equation}
L000725	    -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu \to 0.
L000726	\end{equation}
L000727	For example, for Brownian motion on the half-line, where $p_\mu=e^{-x_0\sqrt{\mu/D}}$, we have
L000728	\begin{equation}
L000729	    -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu = \frac{x_0}{2\sqrt{D\mu}} \to 0.
L000730	\end{equation}
L000731	By contrast, if the intrinsic dynamics impose a strictly positive minimum arrival time $t_*>0$, then the second term does not vanish. Instead,
L000732	\begin{equation}
L000733	    -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu
L000734	    \to
L000735	    t_*.
L000736	\end{equation}
L000737	The simplest example is deterministic first passage: if $\tau\equiv t_*$ (or $\mathbb P(\tau = t) = \delta(t-t_*)$), then $p_\mu=e^{-\mu t_*}$, and hence
L000738	\begin{equation}
L000739	    -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mu}\log p_\mu=t_*.
L000740	\end{equation}
L000741	
L000742	
L000743	
L000744	\section{When DRM outpaces stochastic resetting} \label{outpace}
L000745	Consider again that $\lambda = \alpha r$ and $\mu=r$. The purpose of this section is to provide a detailed derivation of two results discussed in the main text.
L000746	\begin{enumerate}
L000747	    \item In the high-turnover limit, the ratio of the DRM MFPT to the resetting MFPT satisfies,
L000748	    \begin{equation}
L000749	        \lim_{r\to\infty}\frac{\mathbb E[T_{\alpha r,r}]}{\mathbb E[\tau_r]} = \frac{1}{\alpha}.
L000750	    \end{equation}
L000751	    This establishes quite generally that stochastic resetting stops being a lower bound to DRM for any $\alpha>1$. Moreover, there exists a critical value $r^*$, such that for all $r>r^*$, we have $\mathbb E[T_{\alpha r,r}] < \mathbb E[\tau_r]$.
L000752	    \item For the special case of 1D Brownian motion, when the DRM search is sufficiently redundancy dominated, the DRM MFPT becomes a lower bound to the stochastic resetting MFPT for all $r$. More precisely, we have
L000753	    \begin{equation}
L000754	       \mathbb E[\tau_r]  \geq \mathbb E[T_{\alpha r,r}] \quad \textup{for all } \alpha > \phi
L000755	    \end{equation}
L000756	    where $\phi = \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \approx 1.618$ is the golden ratio -- a quantity that emerges in diverse physical and mathematical contexts.
L000757	\end{enumerate}
L000758	
L000759	
L000760	\subsection{When resetting stops being a lower bound to DRM}
L000761	Recall that the DRM MFPT can be described by
L000762	\begin{equation}
L000763	\mathbb E[T_{\lambda,\mu}]
L000764	=
L000765	\int_0^\infty S_{\lambda,\mu}(t)\,\textup{d}t
L000766	\end{equation}
L000767	with $S_{\lambda,\mu}(t)$ as in Eq.~\eqref{final}. For convenience, we define
L000768	\begin{equation}
L000769	q_r
L000770	=
L000771	\int_{0}^{\infty} R_r(t)\,\textup{d}t
L000772	\end{equation}
L000773	with $R_r(t)$ as in Eq.~\eqref{Rmu}. Substitution of $S_{0,r}(t)$ as in Eq.~\eqref{s0mu} into Eq.~\eqref{final} with $\lambda=\alpha r$ yields
L000774	\begin{equation}
L000775	(1-p_r)e^{-\alpha r p_r t}
L000776	\le
L000777	S_{\alpha r,r}(t)
L000778	\le
L000779	e^{\alpha r q_r}(1-p_r+R_r(t))e^{-\alpha r p_r t}.
L000780	\end{equation}
L000781	Integrating over time yields
L000782	\begin{equation}
L000783	\label{Earr}
L000784	\frac{1-p_r}{\alpha r p_r}
L000785	\le
L000786	\mathbb E[T_{\alpha r,r}]
L000787	\le
L000788	e^{\alpha r q_r}
L000789	\left(
L000790	\frac{1-p_r}{\alpha r p_r}+q_r
L000791	\right),
L000792	\end{equation}
L000793	where we used $e^{-\alpha r p_r t} \le 1$ for all $t\ge0$. It remains to show that $q_r$ has only a subleading contribution to Eq.~\eqref{Earr} as $r\to\infty$. Through integration by parts, we have
L000794	\begin{equation}
L000795	q_r=\int_0^\infty t\,e^{-rt}\mathbb{P}(\tau=t)\,\textup{d}t,
L000796	\end{equation}
L000797	and thus
L000798	\begin{equation}
L000799	r q_r=\int_0^\infty rt e^{-rt}\mathbb{P}(\tau=t)\,\textup{d}t.
L000800	\end{equation}
L000801	For any $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $\delta>0$ such that
L000802	\begin{equation}
L000803	\int_0^\delta \mathbb P(\tau=t)\,\text{d}t<\varepsilon.
L000804	\end{equation}
L000805	Thus, for $r>\delta^{-1}$, $rte^{-rt}$ is decreasing on
L000806	$[\delta,\infty)$, and hence
L000807	\begin{align}
L000808	0\le rq_r \le
L000809	e^{-1}\int_0^\delta \mathbb P(\tau=t)\,\textup{d}t +
L000810	r\delta e^{-r\delta}\int_\delta^\infty \mathbb P(\tau=t)\,\textup{d}t \le e^{-1}\varepsilon+r\delta e^{-r\delta}.
L000811	\end{align}
L000812	Taking $r\to\infty$ and then $\varepsilon\to0$ gives $rq_r\to0$. The same cutoff argument gives $p_r\to0$. Since
L000813	$\mathbb E[\tau_r]=(1-p_r)/(rp_r)$, Eq.~\eqref{Earr} implies
L000814	\begin{equation}
L000815	\frac{1}{\alpha} \leq \frac{\mathbb E[T_{\alpha r,r}]}{\mathbb E[\tau_r]} \leq e^{\alpha rq_r}\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\frac{rp_rq_r}{1-p_r}\right).
L000816	\end{equation}
L000817	As $rq_r\to0$ and $p_r\to0$, the right-hand side converges to
L000818	$1/\alpha$. Therefore,
L000819	\begin{equation}
L000820	\lim_{r\to\infty}\frac{\mathbb E[T_{\alpha r,r}]}{\mathbb E[\tau_r]} = \frac{1}{\alpha}.
L000821	\end{equation}
L000822	
L000823	\subsection{When DRM becomes a lower bound to stochastic resetting}
L000824	
L000825	Consider Brownian motion on the half-line with diffusion coefficient $D>0$, initial position $x_0>0$, and a target at the origin. Set
L000826	\begin{equation} \label{zv}
L000827	    z:=x_0\sqrt{\frac{r}{D}},
L000828	    \qquad
L000829	    v:=rt.
L000830	\end{equation}
L000831	The survival probability of a mortal Brownian searcher with mortality rate $r>0$ is given by
L000832	\begin{equation}
L000833	S_{0,r}\!\left(\frac{v}{r}\right)
L000834	=
L000835	1-\frac{e^{-z}}{2}
L000836	\left[
L000837	2-
L000838	\operatorname{erfc}\!\left(\sqrt v-\frac{z}{2\sqrt v}\right)
L000839	+
L000840	e^{2z}\operatorname{erfc}\!\left(\sqrt v+\frac{z}{2\sqrt v}\right)
L000841	\right]
L000842	\label{eq:brownian_mortal_survival}
L000843	\end{equation}
L000844	where $\textup{erfc}(x)$ denotes the complementary error function. For fixed $v>0$,  $S_{0,r}$ has the small-$z$ expansion
L000845	\begin{equation}
L000846	    S_{0,r}\!\left(\frac{v}{r}\right)
L000847	    =
L000848	    z\left[
L000849	    \operatorname{erf}(\sqrt v)+\frac{e^{-v}}{\sqrt{\pi v}}
L000850	    \right]
L000851	    +O(z^2)
L000852	    \label{eq:S0_small_z}
L000853	\end{equation}
L000854	with $\textup{erf}(x)$ denoting the (standard) error function. Taking $\lambda=\alpha r$ and $\mu=r$,
L000855	\begin{equation}
L000856	S_{\alpha r,r}\!\left(\frac{v}{r}\right)
L000857	=
L000858	S_{0,r}\!\left(\frac{v}{r}\right)
L000859	\exp\!\left[-\alpha\int_0^v
L000860	\left(1-S_{0,r}\!\left(\frac{t'}{r}\right)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t'\right].
L000861	\end{equation}
L000862	Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:S0_small_z}, the exponent is $-\alpha v+O(z)$ for fixed $v$, and therefore
L000863	\begin{equation}
L000864	S_{\alpha r,r}\!\left(\frac{v}{r}\right)
L000865	=
L000866	ze^{-\alpha v}
L000867	\left[
L000868	    \operatorname{erf}(\sqrt v)+\frac{e^{-v}}{\sqrt{\pi v}}
L000869	\right]
L000870	+O(z^2).
L000871	\end{equation}
L000872	Integrating over time yields
L000873	\begin{align} \label{eq:brownian_Ialpha}
L000874	    \mathbb{E}[T_{\alpha r,r}]
L000875	    =
L000876	    \frac{z}{r} \int_0^\infty
L000877	    \left[
L000878	    \operatorname{erf}(\sqrt v)+\frac{e^{-v}}{\sqrt{\pi v}}
L000879	    \right]
L000880	    e^{-\alpha v}\,\mathrm{d}v +o\!\left(\frac{z}{r}\right) =
L000881	    \frac{z}{r} \frac{\sqrt{\alpha+1}}{\alpha}+o\!\left(\frac{z}{r}\right)
L000882	\end{align}
L000883	and hence by substitution of $z$ in Eq.~\eqref{zv} we have
L000884	\begin{equation}
L000885	    \lim_{r\to 0} \frac{\sqrt{Dr}}{x_0}\mathbb{E}[T_{\alpha r,r}] = \frac{\sqrt{\alpha+1}}{\alpha}.
L000886	    \label{eq:brownian_low_r_DRM}
L000887	\end{equation}
L000888	Since $p_r = \textup{exp}(-x_0\sqrt{r/D})$ for Brownian motion with rate-$r$ stochastic resetting, it follows that
L000889	\begin{equation}
L000890	    \mathbb{E}[\tau_r]=\frac{1-p_r}{r p_r} \sim
L000891	    \frac{x_0}{\sqrt{Dr}} \label{eq:brownian_low_r_resetting}
L000892	\end{equation}
L000893	as $r\to 0$ and thus
L000894	\begin{equation}
L000895	    \lim_{r\to 0} \frac{\mathbb{E}[T_{\alpha r,r}]}{\mathbb{E}[\tau_r]}
L000896	    =
L000897	    \frac{\sqrt{\alpha+1}}{\alpha}.
L000898	    \label{eq:low_r_ratio}
L000899	\end{equation}
L000900	The leading low-turnover comparison changes sign when the right-hand side of Eq.~\eqref{eq:low_r_ratio} equals one, that is, when
L000901	\begin{equation}
L000902	    \alpha = \phi =\frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}.
L000903	    \label{eq:golden_ratio}
L000904	\end{equation}
L000905	The golden ratio $\phi$ is therefore the low-turnover endpoint of the Brownian phase boundary separating the region where the DRM MFPT outpaces that of stochastic resetting from that in which the resetting MFPT outpaces DRM.
